#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "7a8775a6-f16c-5fc8-865b-89728d73a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-213" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}+6t+1) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}$. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}-5t-6 \\\\ 0 & =(t-6)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1.$ Since $t\\geq 0,$ we discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6.$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=6\\, \\textrm{s}.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}+6\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =-72+90+36+1 \\\\  & =55\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t = 6\\, \\textrm{s}$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a6a4547d-a81a-5c9a-9ab1-2bb20d847fca" :question/math-academy-id "e-217" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t+12) \\\\  & =6t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-6 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "95ddf955-ea59-5ac8-977d-767e46b92bf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6159" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-32t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) <    0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}-32 & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & <32 \\\\ {t}^{3} & <8 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in\\left[ 0, 2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de14590c-5cb1-55d7-a957-286f17bd17e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342166} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fe76ac-5201-5635-a26d-139134840c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342167} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5b4cd9-b88c-59aa-9d54-b8c464a4e2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=e\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342168} {:answer/id #uuid "6d472fbb-2846-5a9b-b80c-7f19d79a6a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342169} {:answer/id #uuid "e23b69c1-b7ec-544c-9288-68b145b0b595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9fe76ac-5201-5635-a26d-139134840c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342167} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae1f563-1213-5317-ba01-40ee04d62d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2aa3e69-2969-5499-b4e6-a1c16d2ad5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109279" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={ln}^{2}⁡t+2,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({ln}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20b8dd4-3ddd-55b3-86fb-83375e88024f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "82692d2c-fd1e-5b01-b3cb-6c8a0df337ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313228} {:answer/id #uuid "e2935a20-1892-5012-9267-697c870c8e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313229} {:answer/id #uuid "327e9d84-65fe-5e64-abf1-9e0e1ec85e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{2}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdadce8-c65a-5ba9-962b-2b2deff8f5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f47d99-0d29-59c2-bb2e-982faa827891" :question/math-academy-id "q-109289" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e30da0-c514-5d6b-a4f2-32b57f53eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313233} {:answer/id #uuid "feb1851d-1cdc-5e66-b8be-5e49f929eb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313234} {:answer/id #uuid "13ed8951-f25a-51bc-80b8-2224b159127c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313235} {:answer/id #uuid "c3741a95-9af5-5d6c-82c3-c2495ede1033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313236} {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b305db0-a24f-59f4-9972-7b09dd35b380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2912d1ed-8c93-531d-8d23-8555cf0ff32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109290" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1910bb6-f3cf-5171-b2b6-2daa5437a3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1910bb6-f3cf-5171-b2b6-2daa5437a3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342173} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d549997-b084-581c-bda3-216acfa1d1be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c2b4319-bc98-5712-ad7c-66778fb352f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2b4319-bc98-5712-ad7c-66778fb352f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342175} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd9cab1d-818b-5061-bc3b-f16d37e13237" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd05cb61-1025-52d9-8f80-565c0086f261" :question/math-academy-id "q-315553" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(5t-15){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-15){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-15){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-10){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-10){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-10){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-5){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5t-5){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 5t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(5\\cdot 1-10){e}^{1}=-5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=1,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-5e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b701c2c8-bb36-5117-92ac-ed5e9ae1e4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342178} {:answer/id #uuid "588f6fb1-a586-5def-97d8-bf3fabd6137d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b701c2c8-bb36-5117-92ac-ed5e9ae1e4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342178} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3861490-cd42-533c-a625-98fe523eb3ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8ef2406-47cb-5949-bfc7-3df8354686c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335684" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}} & =0 \\\\ 8t-7 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58b7edb-43ad-5310-96a1-f4d5e82fc075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58b7edb-43ad-5310-96a1-f4d5e82fc075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342182} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d12f94d-a237-5802-87b3-e74c6daf96ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476287a9-db73-54d5-ae8c-35bc0889d0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335687" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{2}-10t+3,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-10t+3) \\\\  & =4t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-10 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =10 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132f0e53-8a2c-5262-8eeb-c3e9fe48f0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132f0e53-8a2c-5262-8eeb-c3e9fe48f0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342185} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cce752b-5ee1-5083-a80a-796649da076b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a20eefee-8104-50ec-9236-d64413e98bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-335696" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-1 \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc8197e-8bdc-56a0-9784-3ca0da57f8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc8197e-8bdc-56a0-9784-3ca0da57f8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d47248a-4800-5765-9ddb-343d2d3f6d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96a5135-935f-57d6-929f-d12a5564c606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96a5135-935f-57d6-929f-d12a5564c606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342190} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb2e179-4bca-528c-b413-dfdfdbe6cdf2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d78f851-53b4-5dbc-b66a-4ce2e2f325b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335713" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=3t{e}^{-2t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+3t(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(3-6t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (3-6t){e}^{-2t} & <0 \\\\ 3-6t & <0 \\\\ -6t & <-3 \\\\ t & >\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cffe0db4-61fd-5cb1-aa4a-c9db7f51f30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cffe0db4-61fd-5cb1-aa4a-c9db7f51f30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342193} :answer-field/id #uuid "37bd74e5-89bc-50e9-aea2-da380eb0c729" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569216e1-1ce8-59e5-8279-a03ef1a834ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569216e1-1ce8-59e5-8279-a03ef1a834ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342195} :answer-field/id #uuid "361d2fd6-e3c4-5fba-a2a1-5695993a232c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bbdd033-0ced-5dd1-8e93-4f0957d5c8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335894" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6t-6}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(t-1)}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} & <0 \\\\ 3(t-1) & <0 \\\\ t-1 & <0 \\\\ t & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (0,1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0296cc2f-3d1f-5bb4-9d36-9b7d30ca6fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342198} {:answer/id #uuid "31422fc6-6905-5940-b539-138e57462499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31422fc6-6905-5940-b539-138e57462499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342199} :answer-field/id #uuid "207287d5-fe5f-54d4-9e75-b69d3ed6b33c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342197} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e82ee11-5d98-5ed4-a8c5-74ebd6a85861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e82ee11-5d98-5ed4-a8c5-74ebd6a85861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342201} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b7b5179-aec4-5b43-bb47-ed04fb2f4730" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a24f294a-cdfd-53bd-ae89-48540ff56558" :question/math-academy-id "q-335909" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(4t-8){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-8){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-8){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-4){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-4){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-4){e}^{t} \\\\  & =4t\\,{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t\\,{e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 4t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=0.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(0)=(4\\cdot 0-4){e}^{0}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=0,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215e29b1-6f54-59d1-9463-12f9d17a5e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342204} {:answer/id #uuid "4f886dff-b205-5a0c-b8d8-e2edbc2cfbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f886dff-b205-5a0c-b8d8-e2edbc2cfbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342205} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c809dfb-dc43-5522-9c20-cc65e1d2cf6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a048d998-d5e4-5142-9c40-5f93f051b56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{e^4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a048d998-d5e4-5142-9c40-5f93f051b56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{e^4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342207} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c75eb0f-c41d-5aa7-9cb7-c74f7154be79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a437f4ef-d65f-59fa-b5ea-a9457558efc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335910" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(3t-6){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-6){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}+(3t-6)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(9-3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((9-3t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(-3){e}^{-t}+(9-3t)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(3t-12){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3t-12){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 3t-12 & =0 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=4.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(4)=(9-3\\cdot 4){e}^{-4}=-3{e}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=4,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-3{e}^{-4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808c8f3d-93e7-599a-a6d0-425650200bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175045} {:answer/id #uuid "db1dd33b-e016-5116-aec2-6632f88be070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 2)$ and $(3, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175046} {:answer/id #uuid "f6938bc7-5ce8-53a8-aa58-ed003e23859e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175047} {:answer/id #uuid "fd749b77-a715-5978-94b8-7eb25671717b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175048} {:answer/id #uuid "239e3522-01f2-5fe1-8fad-d3d7d96ff819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$ and $(5, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd749b77-a715-5978-94b8-7eb25671717b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175048} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0e2f1f-4609-5672-adf9-8aef0c1741d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e226491-3c89-5e59-9e2d-888677b66097" :question/math-academy-id "q-48909" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a51385-11d7-51c7-a2d4-7659fb60ca51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,s=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293192} {:answer/id #uuid "e5251bd4-427b-5273-8a5d-2b9528d5d26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293193} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb25f5-097c-520e-aaf4-5ab806fd9f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293194} {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} {:answer/id #uuid "7b258107-4669-57fb-b219-823bab2c3396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d351ef-39f4-5dae-94d8-3aeaa48f46a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2ee9a7a-96f8-5c1a-98bc-31a0726aad61" :question/math-academy-id "q-73188" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+8,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time when the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+8) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=1.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=1$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)+8 \\\\  & =1-2+8 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf65e50-bb88-5c21-8317-13393f4c132b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=\\frac{3}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f9421d-8284-5c82-bcd4-0dacb6cfc98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} {:answer/id #uuid "e06fa6db-c8e6-5a00-9d6c-6be9ec3bed60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212} {:answer/id #uuid "8a288eab-e1f2-5e59-9902-802f5d8787ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0710a0c-afe0-53b4-85ec-60d8d04fc9a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8155132e-fb38-5ac5-a729-ddd6db4a43fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313252} {:answer/id #uuid "69b7e53b-44b6-5593-8a6e-deb2504add7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313253} {:answer/id #uuid "df7e3861-6845-507f-a3fe-2ab1edef4223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313254} {:answer/id #uuid "38f0227d-9324-56e2-bff3-5e9ab523f2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee01c62-f6b0-5470-ac4f-67bfcd38591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f742d5d3-d314-55a4-a2e7-77b78815a252" :question/math-academy-id "q-73335" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$"}]}
